          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lawrence-women-in-love-86dea678c8044a4f\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":3,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":2},\"confidence\":0.88,\"analysis\":\"Birkin’s whispered utterance strongly avows love and, in the immediate context of treating himself and Ursula as already married, also affirms their relational union. Its emotional intensity does not independently establish expressive/reflexive force: the narration stresses how much he means by the words rather than presenting them as an unthinking verbal discharge. Some O support remains because the narrator explicitly construes the intended meaning as a self-transcending oneness for which the ordinary subject–object formula “I love you” is inadequate.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The main uncertainty is whether the metaphysical meaning should count as residual core force under O or merely as an unusually elaborate interpretation of T and P.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":\"T captures the avowal and P captures the marital or relational affirmation, but neither fully represents the utterance’s intended invocation of a union in which the separate “I” and “you” have supposedly been transcended.\",\"core_not_context\":\"This is not merely contextual intensity: the narration directly interprets what Birkin means by the words and says that “I love you” is not the real truth because its separate pronouns misrepresent the oneness he intends.\"},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Birkin directly whispers “I love you” to Ursula while kissing her.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The utterance occurs after Birkin tells Ursula that they are effectively already married and receives her presence as a form of resurrection and renewed life. Ursula hears too little in the concise formula and wants more explicit proof, while the narrator attributes to Birkin a profound gratitude and vision of consummated union that she cannot know. The whisper and trembling mark intense delivery, but the text presents the words principally as truthful, densely meant avowal and relational affirmation, not as a reflexive blurt.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I love you,” he whispered as he kissed her\",\"supports\":\"Identifies a direct loving avowal addressed by Birkin to Ursula; the intimate action and delivery support T but do not alone establish E.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"He “trembled with pure hope,” like one “born again.”\",\"supports\":\"Shows the utterance’s exceptional emotional and existential importance while remaining delivery/context evidence rather than independent expressive-reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand Birkin says, “We’re married as much today as we shall be tomorrow.”\",\"supports\":\"Supports substantial performative or undertaking force: the love avowal occurs within his present affirmation of their marital union.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“She could not know how much it meant to him, how much he meant by the few words.”\",\"supports\":\"Presents the utterance as intentional and meaning-laden, favoring T/P over a reflex-like E reading.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration says that even his truthful “I love you” was “not the real truth” but “something beyond love.”\",\"supports\":\"Creates O pressure by explicitly marking the standard love formula as inadequate to Birkin’s intended experience.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e6\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration rejects separate “I” and “you” in favor of “a new, paradisal unit regained from the duality.”\",\"supports\":\"Specifies the residual aspect as self-transcending or mystical union rather than ordinary avowal, undertaking, or affective discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.97}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"strained\",\"diagnosis\":\"T and P naturally capture most of the event, and E is unnecessary despite the passionate delivery. The account is strained by the passage’s explicit claim that the utterance intends a form of subject–object-transcending union that its own grammar cannot state.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":\"Invocation or attempted articulation of self-transcending union beyond the speaker–addressee distinction.\"},\"notes\":\"O is limited to moderate support because the transcendent meaning can still be partly understood as an intensified loving avowal and relational consummation.\"}"
        }
